    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5c48259c-77e1-5cbe-a7ab-45089846128a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(5+x^2){y}^{‴}+1{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)={y}^{′}(3)={y}^{″}(3)=5$\n- **II.** $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x},\\,y(2)=-3$\n- **III.** $(2+x^2){y}^{″}+4{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{‴}+1{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(3)={y}^{′}(3)={y}^{″}(3)=5.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(5+x^2){y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=-3.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
