  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186166815,
      "id": "13e08fd0-4f41-56eb-8650-b72aa739b7a1",
      "title": "Initial Value Problems and Initial Conditions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e65941ac-c117-5c28-964a-ba973e6e763e",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $(4+x^2){y}^{′}+4y={e}^{x}$\n- **II.** $(3+x^2){y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}={e}^{x},\\,y(3)={y}^{′}(3)=-5$\n- **III.** $(3+x^2){y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x},\\,y(3)=-2$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $(3+x^2){y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}={e}^{x}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(3)={y}^{′}(3)=-5.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(3+x^2){y}^{′}+3y={e}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(3)=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "27114f6d-a5a6-5f65-ac3a-3284bc001bfd",
          ":answer-field/key": "I",
